## 1. Introduction
Lipases are very significant enzymes that catalyze a large number of reactions such as hydrolysis, esterification and transesterification [1,2]. These enzymes are the most widely utilized in biocatalysis due to their broad specificity combined with a high region and enantioselectivity and specificity in many cases, their high stability in their different reactions and the reaction spectrum they can catalyze. [3]. However, native lipase suffers from the difficulty of reusability and poor stability which impede more facile and broad commercial uses of lipase. In order to overcome these disadvantages and improve a strong biocatalyst, immobilization of enzymes on solid supports is believed one of the most efficient methods to get better their activity, affinity, selectivity and stability. [2,4–7].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have gained tremendous advances in the past few years as a type of porous material that can be designed with many structural possibilities [5,7–13]. Moreover, MOFs have also attracted considerable research interest as a new platform for immobilizing various enzymes [14–16]. The MOF–enzyme platforms have offered high enzyme-substrate ratios, water-stability, and reusability due to their ease of functionalization, presence of desired hydrophilic/hydrophobic groups, and strong electrostatic interactions with proteins [16,17].
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are a zeolite-like crystal structure in which $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions are coordinated by imidazole derivatives and form a tetrahedral network. Systematic exchange of organic ligand or metal ion results in various 3D structures similar to those of natural zeolites [18]. The materials from the MOF class, having adjustable pore size, are known to show excellent structural diversity, chemical stability and functionality. Due to these properties, ZIFs exhibit great hope for enzyme immobilization [9,10,19–29]. For the first time, Lyu and coworkers [30] introduced ZIF-8 for enzyme immobilization. The authors showed that Cyt c embedded in ZIF-8 a 10-fold increase in peroxidase activity compared to free Cyt c in solution. Recent investigation has shown that ZIF-8, with its high chemical and thermal stability, has unique advantages for enzyme immobilization [14,20,31–33].
Recently, Zhou and co-workers [34] designed by use of ZIF-8 to encapsulate in situ DNA–enzyme composites on the surface of magnetic particles. The thermal, physical and chemical stabilities of horseradish peroxidase have been remarkably enhanced after in situ encapsulation. Zou and group [35], used the monomer of the ZIF-8 as a chemical modification agent for CRL and showed 1.3-times higher catalytic activity compared to the free state of the enzyme. In another example, Cai and co-workers [36], were firstly encapsulated Candida antarctica lipase B onto ZIF-8 and compared with free lipase, the ZIF-8 immobilized lipase exhibited increased thermal and operational stability as well as, resistance to organic solvents.
The structure of solid MOF limits the unfolding of encapsulated enzymes, allowing it to remain stable in organic solvents at high temperatures and in harsh conditions such as unfavorable pH [13,23,37–39]. However, enzymes encapsulated within porous MOFs have difficulties in characterizing structural and conformational changes [13,40]. When used continuously, problems such as insufficient enzyme-substrate interactions and enzyme leakage need to be solved [41–43].
The calix[n]arenes, cone-shaped molecules with a hydrophobic cavity that can accommodate a variety of functional groups, have great potential as protein surface binders [1,44] In addition, calixarenes interact with different functional groups of the enzyme to maintain conformation [1,45]. The calix[4]arene containing carboxyl and phosphonyl groups are highly effective complexing agents [1,44,46], which means that some of these groups interact with the active center of lipase by a combination of hydrogen bonding and electrostatic interactions.
The active site of CRL is hydrophobic on the internal side and hydrophilic on its external face. It is known from our previous studies that calix[n]arene molecules open the lid by making strong hydrogen bonds or inclusion complexes with protein residues [1,47–49]. With this in mind, for the first time, calix[4]arene tetracarboxylic acid was treated with CRL and reacted with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and imidazole by coprecipitation method. Further, the encapsulated lipases were applied for enantioselective hydrolysis of racemic-naproxen methyl ester as a model reaction.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Lipase from CRL (Candida rugosa lipase, type VII), 2- methylimidazole, zinc nitrate hexahydrate $\left(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}099\%\right)$ , $p{-}$ nitrophenyl palmitate (p-NPP), Bradford reagent, S-naproxen, were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich.
## 2.2. Preparation of the CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8
The calix[4]arene tetracarboxylic acid derivative was synthesized according to the published experimental procedures [46,50,51].
Aqueous solution of $Z n(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ (zinc nitrate hexahydrate) $(92.5\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , 4 mL) was added into 2-methylimidazole aqueous solution (2-MIm) $_{\cdot4.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ water), followed by stirring for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ at room temperature. Various amounts of Calix $\mathrm{10{-}40~m g}^{\cdot}$ ) were added to phosphate buffer $\mathrm{70~mM}$ containing CRL $(100~\mathrm{mg})$ ) and mixed for $10\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, then added into a solution of 2-methylimidazole and stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. The mixture turned milky almost instantly after mixing. After the resulting solution was stored at $4^{\circ}C$ for a night, the product was centrifuged $(2028\times g,5\mathrm{min}$ ), washed with deionized water for three times. The resulting encapsulated lipases were lyophilized and stored at $4^{\circ}C$ before use [8,23]. All of the assays were performed in triplicate.
## 2.3. Characterization
The prepared ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were confirmed by using Perkin Elmer brand ATR-FTIR spectrophotometer. Shimadzu UV-1700 Pharma spectrophotometer was used to enzyme activity analysis. The particle size and surface morphology of samples were analyzed by scanning electron microscope (SEM, Jeol, JSM5310, Japan). X-ray diffractions (XRD) of ZIF-8, Calix-ZIF-8, and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were determined on Bruker D8 Advance instrument. Energy Diffuse X-Ray Analysis (EDX) were used to study the surface distribution of the elements.
## 2.4. Enzyme activity assay and protein determination
The activities of CRL, CRL $@$ ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were measured by $p$ -nitrophenyl palmitate ( $p$ -NPP) hydrolysis method according to previous report [49]. A certain amount of free lipase or encapsulated lipase was added into $p$ -NPP solution and mixed for $5\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. The reaction was stopped by the addition of $2{\mathrm{mL}}$ of $0.025\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{C O}_{3}$ solution. The reaction mixture was separated using centrifugation and performed spectroscopically at $405\mathrm{nm}$ using a UV–vis spectrophotometer from the absorbance of the released $p$ -nitrophenol (p-NP). The protein concentration was found according to the Bradford method [52]. All activity experiments were triplicated to obtain the average for more accurate results.
## 2.5. Effect of temperature and pH
The effect of temperature and pH of the CRL, CRL@ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{CRL}@$ Calix-ZIF-8 was incubated at temperatures ranging from $30^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and in different ranges of pH (4.0–9.0) for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Lipase activities were evaluated as in the section above. Each experiment was performed in triplicate.
## 2.6. Thermal and storage stability
The thermal stabilities of CRL, CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were evaluated the residual activities after incubation at $60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ over a period of time.
The storage stabilities of CRL $@$ ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were examined for 5 weeks with a 7-day interval. Subsequently, the residual activity of the encapsulated enzymes was analyzed using above mentioned procedure. All stability measurements were carried out at least in triplicate.
## 2.7. Reusability
The stability of CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 on repeated use was also examined by repeating the $p$ -NPP hydrolysis assay for six consecutive repeats. After each activity determination, the encapsulated lipases were washed with PBS and reintroduced into fresh medium and each experiment was triplicated to obtain more accurate results.
## 2.8. Enantioselective hydrolysis of racemic naproxen methyl ester
Racemic naproxen methyl ester has been prepared according to our previous work [53–55]. The enantioselectivity and conversion of the samples were analyzed by HPLC.
The conversion ${\bf\Pi}({\bf x})$ , enantioselectivity (E), the enantiomeric excess of the product $({\mathrm{ee}}_{\mathrm{p}})$ and the substrate $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ee}}_{s})$ were calculated by using equation [56].
$$
E=\frac{\ln\left[(1-\mathbf{x})(1-\mathbf{e}\mathbf{e}_{s})\right]}{\ln\left[(1-\mathbf{x})(1+\mathbf{e}\mathbf{e}_{s})\right]}
$$
$$
{\bf x}={\frac{{\mathrm{ee}}_{s}}{{\mathrm{ee}}_{s}+{\mathrm{ee}}_{\mathrm{p}}}}\qquad{\bf e}{\bf e}_{s}={\frac{{\bf C}_{\mathrm{R}}-{\bf C}_{\mathrm{S}}}{{\bf C}_{\mathrm{R}}+{\bf C}_{\mathrm{S}}}}.
$$
$$
\mathbf{ee}_{\mathrm{p}}={\frac{{\mathsf{C}}_{\mathrm{S}}-{\mathsf{C}}_{\mathrm{R}}}{{\mathsf{C}}_{\mathrm{S}}+{\mathsf{C}}_{\mathrm{R}}}}
$$
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Preparation of CRL@Calix-ZIF-8
ZIF-8, composed of $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions and 2-methylimidazole (2-MIm), is one typical type of ZIFs [18,25] which exhibits a sodalite-type structure.
Previous studies have reported that for ZIF-8 materials prepared using different zinc nitrate/2-methylimidazole of molar ratio [25,36]. When the molar ratio of zinc nitrate to 2-methylimidazole was 1:40, it was observed that the immobilized enzyme activity was the highest. Therefore, the ZIF-8 synthesis solution with molar ratio of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ : $2\mathrm{-MIm}=1{:}40$ was prepared.
As shown in Scheme 1, using CRL as model enzyme, the preparation of CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 composites was carried out by first mixing CRL solution and Calix. Then, the mixture was added zinc nitrate aqueous solution, and 2-methylimidazole aqueous solution at room temperature. The preparation of CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 composites was performed in a single step.
The Candida rugosa lipase has an active site consisting of 31 amino acids with a lid having a catalytic triad of glutamine, histidine and serine (Glu-His-Ser) [47]. The inner surface of lid is hydrophilic and the outer surface is hydrophobic and can have two different conformations, open and closed [45,57]. The carboxyl groups of calix[4]arene form complexes with the lipase containing lysine, thiol, imidazole and indole residues or with strong hydrogen bonds to open up the lid [1,46–49,57]. The encapsulation into the metal organic framework formed by the addition of 2-methylimidazole and zinc nitrate solution on the CRL-Calix complex provides the immobility and stability of the enzyme.
Different proportions of Calix/CRL were used to design ZIF-8 and the highest activity $(481\mathrm{U/g})$ was obtained when 0.2 ratio of Calix/CRL was taken. This result is not surprising because while the Calix/CRL ratio increases, Calix complexes with amino acids in the active sites of enzyme, causing a decrease in activity [1,44] (Fig. 1).
Table 1 summarizes the results of our research on ZIF-8-lipase platforms. In this study, from Table 1, the enzyme loading values were obtained to be $240~\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $312\mathrm{mg/g}$ for CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{CRL}@$ ZIF-8. Also, catalytic activities were 481 $\mathrm{U}/\mathrm{g}$ (CRL@Calix-ZIF-8) and
$241~\mathrm{U/mg}$ (CRL@ZIF-8). The enzyme loading data of CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 was 1.3 times higher than that of the CRL@ZIF-8. The researchers found that the decrease in enzyme activity after immobilization could be due to two reasons: (i) conformational changes that occur during process immobilization of enzymes, and (ii) mass transfer restriction resistance by the framework around enzyme molecules [62]. Generally, an amphiphilic polypeptide chain called the ‘lid’, that in the closed conformation covers the active site, moves allowing the substrate to bind to the catalytic triad in the active site [61]. Thus, the carboxyl groups of calix[4]arene form complexes with the lipase contains free lysine, thiol, imidazole, and indole residue groups or with strong hydrogen bonds to open up the lid. The high activity results obtained showed that the structure of the enzymes was preserved, and that high loading efficiency could be achieved. It is important that the framework around CRL-Calix embedded in ZIF-8 is in active conformation.
## 3.2. Characterizations
The FTIR spectrum of Calix, ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 was demonstrated in Fig. 2. The characteristic absorption bands of ZIF-8 for the stretching $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ band of the imidazole ring and $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ stretching vibration were observed at $1567~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $420~\mathrm{{cm}^{-}~^{1}}$ , respectively. The stretches carboxylate bands of $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ at $1650\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were found in spectra of CRL@Calix-ZIF-8, which corresponded to amide I mainly from $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ stretching mode. Further, the strong band at $758~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is due to out-of-plane bending of the imidazole aromatic ring [25,58].
The SEM images of the ZIF-8, CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were illustrated in Fig. 3. The SEM image reveals that ZIF-8 had a uniform particle size of about $400\mathrm{nm}$ with smooth surface. The CRL located at the outer ZIF-8 surface can be clearly monitored around the CRL $@$ -ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 composite. It has been reported that the size and shape of the prepared MOF-biomolecule composites mainly depend on the immobilization method and the applied biomacromolecules [9,63]. Furthermore, the EDX elemental analysis of CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 was performed and displays the presence of Zn as component of ZIF-8 (Fig. 4).
The XRD patterns of ZIF-8, Calix-ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were illustrated in Fig. 5. The characteristic peaks at $2\uptheta=7.2^{\circ}$ , $10.6^{\circ}$ , $12.9^{\circ}$ , $14.9^{\circ}$ , 16.6°, $18.2^{\circ}$ , $19.7^{\circ}$ , $22.3^{\circ}$ , $24.7^{\circ}$ , $25.4^{\circ}$ , $26.9^{\circ}$ , $30.8^{\circ}$ , $31,7^{\circ}$ were observed for pure ZIF-8 [20,60]. It could be clearly seen that the crystal structures Calix-ZIF-8 were not significantly different from ZIF-8. Because the positions of all diffraction peaks matched well. However, it was observed that some of the peaks decreased in intensity with the introduction of the enzyme into the structure. Similarly, the characteristic peaks of the CRL-loaded ZIF-8 gradually weakened, and more pronounced broad scattered peaks appeared [24,35].
## 3.3. Effect of pH and temperature
The highest activity of free lipase at $\mathsf{p H}7.0$ , while both CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 at $\mathrm{pH}8.0$ were presented in Fig. 6. The pH activity profile of the CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 was broader than those of the free lipase and showed better pH stability [29,64]. Furthermore, The CRL@CalixZIF-8 exhibited enhanced resistance to more alkali values. The pH shift relates on the immobilization method and the surface charges of the carrier [65,66]. It may also be from hydrogen bonding and ionic interactions between lipase and Calix. Samui et al. [41] obtained highest activity for CRL immobilized on Zn-( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC) metal-organic frameworks (MOFs).
Fig. 7 illustrates the results of effect of temperature on the activity of free lipase and CRL@ZIF-8, CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 in the range $30{\mathrm{-}}60^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ Both the free and immobilized lipases indicated the maximum catalytic activity at $35^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ Meanwhile, free lipase and immobilized lipases showed similar temperature, which meant they did not alter enzyme properties by immobilization.
## 3.4. Thermal and storage stability
Thermal stability is very important for an immobilization method developed. Free lipase and CRL@ZIF-8, CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were incubated at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ and lipase activity was measured at various time intervals, as shown in Fig. 8. The free lipase lost its catalytic activity completely at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ after $100~\mathrm{{min}}$ , while CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@CalixZIF-8 retained approximately 73 and $84\%$ , respectively. The interactions between the enzyme and Calix during the immobilization process can be explained by the enzyme's protection of its tertiary structure [1].
The storage stabilities of CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were examined in PBS at $4^{\circ}C$ for 5 weeks with a 7-day interval (Fig. 9). After five weeks of storage, CRL@Calix-ZIF-8-and CRL $@$ ZIF-8 displayed that the residual activity 73 and $50\%$ , respectively. So, CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 showed minimal structural degradation of enzyme and preserved its active conformation under storage conditions. Increased storage stability may be due to the protective effect of Calix around enzyme molecules encapsulating into ZIF-8, resulting in minimum possible degradation mostly caused by the chemical environment (buffer solution) in the active sites of the enzyme [67,68]. CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 exhibited to have excellent durability potential for industrial applications.
## 3.5. Reusability
The reusability of enzymes is of great importance for practical and industrial applications [29,66]. The CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 still retained $67\%$ and $83\%$ of their activities after the 6 reuses (Fig. 10). The improved recyclability of CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 was mainly caused by the prevention of enzyme desorption, since ZIF-8 framework and lipase molecule formed a strong interaction [58].
These data confirmed that the minimal decrease in activity of immobilized lipases was absent of enzyme leakage and the enzyme retained its conformation during six repetitive cycles.
3.6. Michaelis–Menten kinetic parameters of CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@CalixZIF-8
The maximum reaction velocity $(\mathsf{V}_{\mathrm{max}})$ and Michaelis constant $(\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}})$ of CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were determined by measuring initial rates by varying amounts of $p$ -nitrophenyl palmitate substrate. The kinetic behaviors of CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 were compared (Table 2). As shown in Table 2, the $\mathtt{V}_{\mathrm{max}}$ of CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@CalixZIF-8 were 227 (U/mg protein), and 500 $\mathrm{{\Delta}U/g}$ protein), respectively. Besides, the $\mathtt{V}_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of CRL $@$ Calix-ZIF-8 was 2.2-fold higher than the CRL-ZIF-8. This could be probably due to the framework around CRLCalix embedded in ZIF-8 is in active conformation [64]. Also, the CRL $@$ Calix-ZIF-8 of $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ value was higher than the CRL@ZIF-8, which indicates that the affinity of the enzyme to the substrate was lowered [35,69].
## 3.7. Enantioselectivity assay
Researchers can use the enzymatic kinetic resolution of racemic mixtures to get enantiopure derivatives via chiral chemical synthesis. These enantiopure compounds are frequently used in the pharmaceutical industry. Many studies have shown that it is more effective to obtain higher purity enantiomers using lipase than traditional chemical methods [1,45,46,48,55,70]. However, there is no other study reported about the Calix-MOF platform so far and its catalytic activities against enantioselective hydrolysis reactions. In view of this point, in the present study, the kinetic resolution of the CRL@ZIF-8 and CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 was examined for enantioselective hydrolysis of racemic naproxen methyl ester. According to the results in Table 3, it was found that CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 has high enantioselectivity $\mathrm{{\dot{E}=183}}$ ) as compared to the CRL@ZIF-8.
## 4. Conclusions
In supramolecular chemistry, calixarenes represent the most important macrocyclic host molecules. In present work, first time, calix[4] arene tetracarboxylic acid was used into preparation of ZIF-lipase encapsulation. For this purpose, calix[4]arene derivative was first treated with CRL and reacted with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and imidazole by co-precipitation. CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 was obtained via one step method and compared to CRL $@$ ZIF-8. The prepared CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 exhibited 2-fold improved catalytic activity with respect to CRL $@$ ZIF-8 (without Calix support). The CRL-Calix complex provides the immobility and stability of the lipase. It was observed that the CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 showed an excellent recycle performance that can retain $83\%$ of its initial activity even after being reused 6 times. Furthermore, the calix-enzyme embedded in ZIF-8 was observed to have higher stability towards temperature, neutral or alkaline conditions, and long-term storage. The CRL@Calix-ZIF-8 was applied as an effective biocatalyst for enantioselective hydrolysis of racemic naproxen methyl ester. This work predicts that with such an easy, fast and one-pot enzyme immobilization technique, it will open new ways for developing significant biocatalysts with excellent catalytic properties and exploring new horizons for enzymes in pharmaceutical industry.
